## F2. What this does not show

- **Not a test on unseen data.** D5 used these same 9 cases. What guards against tuning is
  that the rule has nothing to tune and each algorithm ran once per case. A stronger test
  would run the same code on task samples nobody has looked at.
- **Not real electricity.** The simulator multiplies time by SPECpower watts for the CPU a
  task *requested*. Real servers also use power for memory, disks, fans and cooling, and
  real tasks rarely use exactly what they request.
- **Not other days.** Alibaba published one 12-hour window, so this shows the rule on these
  jobs, not on a different day, season or company.
- **Not independent repeats.** The three sizes are nested (the 250 groups are part of the
  1,000, which are part of the 4,000), and the three server models replay the same tasks.
  The 9 cases are 9 views of one sample, so no statistical significance is claimed.
- **Pressure is a warning sign, not slowness.** In this simulator a crowded server does not
  slow down, because requests can never exceed capacity.
- **The rule needs job ids.** It works because every task in the trace says which job it
  belongs to. A system without that information could not use it.
